1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,205
Why were burnt rice grains found in the ruins of Nalanda?

2
00:00:03,205 --> 00:00:06,766
The question seems small, but a whole story is hidden in it.

3
00:00:06,766 --> 00:00:09,971
Under the soil of Bihar, clay jars were found.

4
00:00:09,971 --> 00:00:13,177
Inside them were grains of rice. Black, like coal,

5
00:00:13,177 --> 00:00:15,669
and very old. Not in any kitchen,

6
00:00:15,669 --> 00:00:17,450
but in the ruins of a school.

7
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:21,234
These grains were dug out by the ASI. ASI means the Archaeological Survey of India,

8
00:00:21,234 --> 00:00:24,675
the government body that excavates old sites.

9
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:27,427
According to them, this rice is from the 12th century.

10
00:00:27,427 --> 00:00:30,868
Even today it is kept in the Nalanda site museum.

11
00:00:30,868 --> 00:00:33,620
It was not burnt for cooking.

12
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:35,340
It was burnt by fire.

13
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:39,589
You may have heard that the Nalanda library kept burning for 3 months.

14
00:00:39,589 --> 00:00:42,858
On WhatsApp, in reels, this claim is going around everywhere.

15
00:00:42,858 --> 00:00:45,800
But is it written in any old text?

16
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,088
Or is it a story added later?

17
00:00:48,088 --> 00:00:51,030
In this video we will find exactly that, with proof.

18
00:00:51,030 --> 00:00:53,304
We will go from the texts of Tibet,

19
00:00:53,304 --> 00:00:57,472
to the account of a traveler from China. Then the ASI excavation,

20
00:00:57,472 --> 00:01:00,882
layers of ash, and a book of Persian history.

21
00:01:00,882 --> 00:01:05,051
At every step we will say clearly what the text says,

22
00:01:05,051 --> 00:01:08,840
what has been found in the soil, and what is only folklore.

23
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:12,865
First, let's go to the Nalanda that the texts remembered.

24
00:01:12,865 --> 00:01:16,890
Nalanda was a mahavihara. Mahavihara means a big Buddhist monastery and school,

25
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:20,183
where monks both lived and studied.

26
00:01:20,183 --> 00:01:22,745
Students came from far away to learn here.

27
00:01:22,745 --> 00:01:26,770
It was counted among the most famous places of its time.

28
00:01:26,770 --> 00:01:29,718
A traveler came from China, named Xuanzang,

29
00:01:29,718 --> 00:01:31,930
who is also called Hiuen Tsang.

30
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:34,878
He reached Nalanda after a long and difficult journey.

31
00:01:34,878 --> 00:01:38,932
He stayed here for 5 years. He studied Buddhist logic and philosophy,

32
00:01:38,932 --> 00:01:41,512
and also learned Panini's Sanskrit grammar.

33
00:01:41,512 --> 00:01:44,460
The accounts he wrote are still read today.

34
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:48,258
Xuanzang learned Yoga shastra from his teacher Shilabhadra. Just imagine,

35
00:01:48,258 --> 00:01:52,436
a foreign student, by the light of a lamp, reading manuscripts.

36
00:01:52,436 --> 00:01:54,714
Pothi means a handwritten book.

37
00:01:54,714 --> 00:01:58,512
In those days every book was written by hand.

38
00:01:58,512 --> 00:02:02,310
These very manuscripts later become the heart of this story.

39
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:06,918
Tibetan history texts mention the name of a library, Dharmaganja.

40
00:02:06,918 --> 00:02:09,431
It means, the marketplace of dharma.

41
00:02:09,431 --> 00:02:14,877
This is found in the book of a Tibetan scholar named Sumpa Khenpo.

42
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:18,647
It describes several large multi-storey buildings.

43
00:02:18,647 --> 00:02:21,160
Their names were Ratnasagara, Ratnodadhi and Ratnaranjaka.

44
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,915
According to these texts, Ratnodadhi was 9 storeys high.

45
00:02:24,915 --> 00:02:28,670
The most sacred manuscripts were kept in it, like the Prajnaparamita Sutra.

46
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:31,673
But remember, this is what the text says.

47
00:02:31,673 --> 00:02:36,930
So far, no such 9-storey building has come out of the ground in the excavations.

48
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:39,764
Now imagine, it is night. A tall building,

49
00:02:39,764 --> 00:02:42,598
the light of lamps coming from the windows.

50
00:02:42,598 --> 00:02:46,141
Inside, monks are quietly copying manuscripts and looking after them.

51
00:02:46,141 --> 00:02:49,329
This picture comes to our mind from the texts.

52
00:02:49,329 --> 00:02:50,392
Now let's see

53
00:02:50,392 --> 00:02:53,580
what the soil says about this picture.

54
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,079
The text is one thing, the soil is another.

55
00:02:56,079 --> 00:03:00,362
Archaeology means the science of digging the earth to find traces of ancient people.

56
00:03:00,362 --> 00:03:03,931
The ruins of Nalanda stayed buried under the soil for centuries.

57
00:03:03,931 --> 00:03:07,501
From outside, they looked like just tall mounds of earth.

58
00:03:07,501 --> 00:03:11,070
No one had any idea what was hidden underneath.

59
00:03:11,070 --> 00:03:14,635
Then the ASI began proper excavation here.

60
00:03:14,635 --> 00:03:17,755
This work went on from 1915 to 1937.

61
00:03:17,755 --> 00:03:22,658
Workers and archaeologists together removed the soil, layer by layer.

62
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:27,115
Slowly, walls of red bricks began to rise from below.

63
00:03:27,115 --> 00:03:30,680
As if a city was waking up from sleep.

64
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,094
What emerged was not like some small monastery.

65
00:03:34,094 --> 00:03:36,749
There were several viharas built in a straight line,

66
00:03:36,749 --> 00:03:39,404
and in front of them, large brick temples.

67
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:42,818
Everything was built on a well-thought-out plan.

68
00:03:42,818 --> 00:03:45,853
As if someone had first made the whole plan,

69
00:03:45,853 --> 00:03:47,370
and only then laid the bricks.

70
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:50,166
A vihara means the building where monks lived.

71
00:03:50,166 --> 00:03:53,662
An open courtyard in the middle, and small rooms all around.

72
00:03:53,662 --> 00:03:57,158
In these very rooms the monks lived, studied and slept.

73
00:03:57,158 --> 00:03:59,604
Today, when you stand in them,

74
00:03:59,604 --> 00:04:03,100
it feels as if someone will walk in any moment carrying a manuscript.

75
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:06,935
And then the excavators saw something else. Black soil.

76
00:04:06,935 --> 00:04:10,073
A thick layer of ash. Burnt wooden beams.

77
00:04:10,073 --> 00:04:13,211
Bricks whose colour had changed from heat.

78
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:16,698
All of this was testifying to one thing.

79
00:04:16,698 --> 00:04:18,790
A great fire once broke out here.

80
00:04:18,790 --> 00:04:21,567
According to the ASI, 11 viharas were found here,

81
00:04:21,567 --> 00:04:23,947
and many large brick temples.

82
00:04:23,947 --> 00:04:27,913
All of these were arranged along a central line.

83
00:04:27,913 --> 00:04:30,293
So Nalanda was not just one building.

84
00:04:30,293 --> 00:04:33,863
It was a campus like an entire city, where study,

85
00:04:33,863 --> 00:04:36,640
worship and living all went on together.

86
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:39,939
In the first monastery that the ASI excavated,

87
00:04:39,939 --> 00:04:43,972
stratigraphy was done. Stratigraphy means reading the layers of soil,

88
00:04:43,972 --> 00:04:46,171
just like the pages of a book.

89
00:04:46,171 --> 00:04:49,471
The lower layer is older, the upper one is newer.

90
00:04:49,471 --> 00:04:52,770
Each layer tells the story of a different era.

91
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:56,244
In this monastery, 9 different periods of construction were found. This means,

92
00:04:56,244 --> 00:04:59,401
this monastery was built, broken down, and rebuilt again and again.

93
00:04:59,401 --> 00:05:02,559
Each generation raised a new wall on top of the old wall.

94
00:05:02,559 --> 00:05:04,454
Just like a house gets repaired,

95
00:05:04,454 --> 00:05:06,980
in the same way work went on here for centuries.

96
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:10,596
This is very important. Nalanda was not built in a day.

97
00:05:10,596 --> 00:05:12,568
And from this evidence it seems,

98
00:05:12,568 --> 00:05:15,198
perhaps it did not end in a single day either.

99
00:05:15,198 --> 00:05:17,499
For centuries people kept repairing it here.

100
00:05:17,499 --> 00:05:19,471
This means damage must have come before too,

101
00:05:19,471 --> 00:05:22,430
and people must have stood up again and put it back together.

102
00:05:22,430 --> 00:05:25,924
Now let's go back to those burnt rice grains. Rice kept in a jar,

103
00:05:25,924 --> 00:05:27,829
which turned black in the fire.

104
00:05:27,829 --> 00:05:31,005
These samples are still kept today in the ASI museum,

105
00:05:31,005 --> 00:05:33,229
along with copper plates, inscriptions and coins.

106
00:05:33,229 --> 00:05:37,040
This is evidence that you can see with your own eyes.

107
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:41,872
According to the ASI, this ash is a sign of a large, widespread burning.

108
00:05:41,872 --> 00:05:45,323
So the fire really did happen. This is not a story,

109
00:05:45,323 --> 00:05:49,119
it is proof from the soil. But how many days the fire lasted, who set it,

110
00:05:49,119 --> 00:05:52,570
the soil does not tell. This is where the debate begins.

111
00:05:52,570 --> 00:05:55,196
Now let's come to the most famous claims,

112
00:05:55,196 --> 00:05:58,197
that you hear again and again. One claim,

113
00:05:58,197 --> 00:06:01,573
that the library kept burning for months. And another claim,

114
00:06:01,573 --> 00:06:04,199
that there were countless books there. Let's see,

115
00:06:04,199 --> 00:06:06,824
what evidence lies behind these claims,

116
00:06:06,824 --> 00:06:08,700
and what historians say.

117
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:11,977
It is said that Nalanda had 90 lakh manuscripts.

118
00:06:11,977 --> 00:06:14,890
But scholars like historian D.N. Jha say,

119
00:06:14,890 --> 00:06:18,532
this is just a number. No list from that time,

120
00:06:18,532 --> 00:06:21,445
no register has been found. What the real number was,

121
00:06:21,445 --> 00:06:23,630
No one knows this even today.

122
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:27,309
On the other hand, some people say there were such tall and big buildings,

123
00:06:27,309 --> 00:06:29,316
so there must have been a lot of manuscripts too.

124
00:06:29,316 --> 00:06:31,322
That makes sense.

125
00:06:31,322 --> 00:06:35,001
But having a lot and having a fixed count are different things.

126
00:06:35,001 --> 00:06:38,680
The texts mention the buildings, but there is no proof of a count.

127
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,344
Now, the 3 months claim. Historians say,

128
00:06:41,344 --> 00:06:45,339
the main text of that period mentions the vihara being destroyed.

129
00:06:45,339 --> 00:06:48,669
But it does not mention it burning for 3 months.

130
00:06:48,669 --> 00:06:51,665
That figure comes from later folk tales,

131
00:06:51,665 --> 00:06:53,330
not from the old written histories.

132
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:55,185
A chronicle means a diary of history,

133
00:06:55,185 --> 00:06:58,895
written during the time of a king or an army.

134
00:06:58,895 --> 00:07:01,491
One such book is the Tabaqat-i Nasiri.

135
00:07:01,491 --> 00:07:03,717
It was written by the Persian historian Minhaj-i-Siraj.

136
00:07:03,717 --> 00:07:08,540
It mentions an attack on a fort-like vihara in Bihar.

137
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:12,143
Minhaj writes that Bakhtiyar Khalji's army captured that place.

138
00:07:12,143 --> 00:07:13,644
A great many books were found there.

139
00:07:13,644 --> 00:07:17,847
The army looked for people who could tell what was written in the books.

140
00:07:17,847 --> 00:07:19,048
But the text says,

141
00:07:19,048 --> 00:07:21,750
the people who could read them had already been killed.

142
00:07:21,750 --> 00:07:24,929
And this is where historians get stuck on one point.

143
00:07:24,929 --> 00:07:27,755
The book just calls that place Bihar.

144
00:07:27,755 --> 00:07:30,935
Some scholars believe this was Nalanda. D.N.

145
00:07:30,935 --> 00:07:32,701
Jha and other historians say,

146
00:07:32,701 --> 00:07:35,880
it could also be the nearby Odantapuri vihara.

147
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:39,090
Odantapuri was also a big mahavihara, close to Nalanda.

148
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:41,016
It too was as strong as a fort.

149
00:07:41,016 --> 00:07:44,548
So the book's description could fit it as well.

150
00:07:44,548 --> 00:07:47,437
Meaning, the proof of fire at Nalanda is in the soil.

151
00:07:47,437 --> 00:07:49,363
But in which attack, on which day,

152
00:07:49,363 --> 00:07:51,610
is still a matter of debate.

153
00:07:51,610 --> 00:07:55,240
Now listen to another story, which comes from Tibet.

154
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:57,054
This is not history, it is a legend,

155
00:07:57,054 --> 00:08:01,047
meaning a folk tale with no solid proof to hold on to.

156
00:08:01,047 --> 00:08:06,491
But it shows how people kept remembering this fire for centuries,

157
00:08:06,491 --> 00:08:07,580
and kept telling it.

158
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:13,442
The Tibetan writer Taranatha wrote that some insulted sadhus did penance for 9 years.

159
00:08:13,442 --> 00:08:15,786
When the penance was complete, they returned.

160
00:08:15,786 --> 00:08:19,304
They scattered ash filled with mantras all around.

161
00:08:19,304 --> 00:08:20,867
And the story says,

162
00:08:20,867 --> 00:08:23,993
from that very ash, a fire started in the library.

163
00:08:23,993 --> 00:08:27,510
The story has magic, but the pain feels real.

164
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:29,209
Now, an eyewitness account.

165
00:08:29,209 --> 00:08:31,927
The Tibetan monk Dharmasvamin reached Nalanda in 1235.

166
00:08:31,927 --> 00:08:34,985
This was many years after the attack.

167
00:08:34,985 --> 00:08:37,364
He saw that Nalanda was broken down,

168
00:08:37,364 --> 00:08:39,742
but it had not been completely destroyed.

169
00:08:39,742 --> 00:08:42,800
This account can still be found in his biography today.

170
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:46,058
There, the abbot Rahula Shribhadra was still teaching.

171
00:08:46,058 --> 00:08:48,955
Abbot means the head of a monastery. Among the ruins,

172
00:08:48,955 --> 00:08:52,213
some students sat in front of him. The text says,

173
00:08:52,213 --> 00:08:56,558
Nalanda was now just a shadow of its former self.

174
00:08:56,558 --> 00:08:58,730
Even so, the teaching was going on.

175
00:08:58,730 --> 00:09:01,478
This account changes the story.

176
00:09:01,478 --> 00:09:05,012
If studies were still going on here in 1235,

177
00:09:05,012 --> 00:09:09,331
then Nalanda did not end in one blow. Perhaps slowly,

178
00:09:09,331 --> 00:09:12,472
loss after loss, it kept emptying out.

179
00:09:12,472 --> 00:09:15,220
Some historians point in this same direction.

180
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:19,047
So what do we really know so far? Nalanda existed,

181
00:09:19,047 --> 00:09:22,108
it was very big, and it did catch fire.

182
00:09:22,108 --> 00:09:24,787
The soil and the texts, both tell us this.

183
00:09:24,787 --> 00:09:27,466
But some questions are still open today.

184
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:30,910
And maybe this is Nalanda's biggest mystery.

185
00:09:30,910 --> 00:09:34,233
The biggest question: where was Dharmaganja? Its name is in the texts,

186
00:09:34,233 --> 00:09:35,895
and tall buildings are mentioned.

187
00:09:35,895 --> 00:09:39,550
But in the excavated area, it has still not been settled

188
00:09:39,550 --> 00:09:42,541
exactly where the library buildings stood.

189
00:09:42,541 --> 00:09:45,200
Maybe it is still under the soil.

190
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:47,616
And one more question: which manuscripts were lost?

191
00:09:47,616 --> 00:09:49,686
No complete list of them survived.

192
00:09:49,686 --> 00:09:53,483
We don't know what was written on those shelves.

193
00:09:53,483 --> 00:09:57,279
What survived is the knowledge that travelers like Xuanzang learned and carried away.

194
00:09:57,279 --> 00:10:00,730
What stayed here, we don't even know its names.

195
00:10:00,730 --> 00:10:03,385
Today you can visit Nalanda, Bihar.

196
00:10:03,385 --> 00:10:06,420
UNESCO has recognized it as a World Heritage Site,

197
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:10,213
meaning a shared heritage of the whole world. Monasteries of red brick,

198
00:10:10,213 --> 00:10:13,627
tall temples and long paths still stand today.

199
00:10:13,627 --> 00:10:17,420
In the morning sunlight, this place looks different.

200
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:20,229
In the site museum, do go and see that burnt rice,

201
00:10:20,229 --> 00:10:23,038
...which were the beginnings of this story. Copper plates,

202
00:10:23,038 --> 00:10:25,847
coins and clay pots are there too.

203
00:10:25,847 --> 00:10:29,008
Then stand inside a monastery room and think.

204
00:10:29,008 --> 00:10:32,870
Right here, a monk once read his manuscript by the light of a lamp.

205
00:10:32,870 --> 00:10:35,858
And when you see a black mark on a wall,

206
00:10:35,858 --> 00:10:37,517
pause for a moment.

207
00:10:37,517 --> 00:10:40,505
It may be a memory of that very fire.

208
00:10:40,505 --> 00:10:42,829
Do listen to the guide's story too, you'll enjoy it.

209
00:10:42,829 --> 00:10:46,480
But along with it, ask one question: what is the proof?

210
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:49,408
Next time we will go in search of Odantapuri.

211
00:10:49,408 --> 00:10:53,067
That mahavihara whose name came up again and again in this story,

212
00:10:53,067 --> 00:10:55,995
but whose location is still debated today.

213
00:10:55,995 --> 00:10:58,922
Was it really near Nalanda?

214
00:10:58,922 --> 00:11:01,850
What has been found there? We'll look at it with the evidence.

215
00:11:01,850 --> 00:11:04,216
Now, an easy question for you.

216
00:11:04,216 --> 00:11:09,422
Where did you first hear about Nalanda burning for 3 months,

217
00:11:09,422 --> 00:11:12,735
school, home or WhatsApp? Tell us in the comments.

218
00:11:12,735 --> 00:11:15,574
And if you have been to Nalanda,

219
00:11:15,574 --> 00:11:19,833
then tell us what you remember the most from there.
